  "scope": "Common application work, native Rust CBOR versus original C++ binary; no bridge. Same frozen client, 64 cyclic 1027-byte CBOR byte-string values (1024 application bytes), 16-message windows, two private pairs and 8192 other idle rooms. C++ treats values as opaque; Rust validates CBOR. Each case runs fresh in a private net namespace, sequentially, pinned to the same server CPU set with client cores separate. C++ loop count is unchanged (one per hardware thread), Rust worker count explicit. Case private: only private traffic. Case churn: private traffic plus eight fixed-room reconnect workers; every welcome, self delivery, Close/EOF checked, and C++ retired member counts verified before its first 30s GC. Case query: private traffic plus two authenticated channel inspectors at10ms each, no churn; names/counts and full replies verified, sorting normalized only for C++ which does not promise it. HTTP management has no C++ counterpart. Setup and cleanup are gated outside timing, every normal close counted, Rust counters/bytes/reservations checked. Latencies include client validation and concurrent client tasks, logarithmic percentile bucket upper bounds. RSS sampled at gates, not continuous peak/allocated memory. Server/client CPU covers all mixed work. Original shipped C++ ELF has unknown optimization flags and isolated old dependency versions. Plaintext loopback, no TLS, rates/heartbeats off, default Rust queues; not a language-only or production-capacity comparison.",
